# T-033: on this machine, core.autocrlf=true + no .gitattributes meant a
# fresh `git clone` of this repo materialised every tracked text file as
# CRLF, one byte per line heavier than the LF blob actually stored — while
# a working tree that never round-tripped through an actual checkout (e.g.
# a file written by a script and `git add`ed directly) stayed LF. Same
# commit, same clean `git status` in both, byte-for-byte different files.
# scripts/ia-grade.mjs's corpus digest (a raw sha256 over file bytes, by
# design — see that file's own comments on why rates are graded from
# JSON.parse, which does not care about line endings, while the digest that
# claims "same corpus" deliberately does) surfaced it first, but the same
# mismatch applies to every tracked text file, not just the corpus.
#
# `eol=lf` makes a checkout emit LF regardless of core.autocrlf, so a fresh
# clone now materialises the same bytes the repository already stores — no
# blob content changes (T-033 measured the stored blobs as already LF; only
# the checkout step was converting), so this is a checkout-policy file, not
# a renormalisation.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary
